	abstract = {The Galapagos Islands are a mosaic of scrub to cloud forest, pasture to native grasslands, and lava fields to herbaceous vegetation, predominately defined through life zones that are set through topography, climate, soils, and human engagement. While the challenges to the assessment and monitoring of island ecosystems are many, the role of remote sensing has proven vital to their characterization. Direct mapping of individual animals (e.g., giant tortoises) and animal colonies (e.g., sea-lions) is scale dependent relative to the animals being characterized and the spatial and spectral resolutions of the remote sensing systems used for assessments. In this chapter, we provide a context to the forces of change affecting the Galapagos Islands, a literature review of important methodologies and mapping technologies to assess land cover/land use change, a case study analysis of the Galapagos giant tortoise and landscape dynamics, and a discussion of the forces that influence animal behaviors and populations as well as ecosystem dynamics that shape landscape change. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
